Configuring Role-Based Access for Need-to-Know
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Create PostgreSQL roles representing HR, finance, and audit functions
Implement column-level and row-level security to enforce need-to-know
Enable pgAudit to log access attempts and privilege usage
Validate segregation of duties by impersonating each role
Map the resulting access model to PDPL (Personal Data Protection Law) data-sharing requirements
Prerequisites
Basic SQL knowledge (SELECT, GRANT, CREATE ROLE)
Familiarity with Linux command line and package managers
Understanding of RBAC concepts (roles vs users, least privilege)
No prior pgAudit experience required
Environment Setup
Your Al Nafi Linux machine comes with terminal access. Install PostgreSQL and pgAudit:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y postgresql postgresql-contrib
sudo apt install -y postgresql-15-pgaudit   # adjust version if different
sudo systemctl start postgresql
sudo systemctl enable postgresql
Verify installation:

psql --version
sudo -u postgres psql -c "SELECT version();"
Create a working database:

sudo -u postgres createdb agency_records
