Task 2: Apply Column-Level and Row-Level Security
Column-level security: Revoke default access, then grant selectively.

REVOKE ALL ON employee_records FROM PUBLIC;

-- TODO: Grant hr_role SELECT on all columns
-- TODO: Grant finance_role SELECT only on (emp_id, full_name, department, salary)
-- TODO: Grant audit_role SELECT only on (emp_id, department, audit_flag)

GRANT SELECT (emp_id, full_name, department, salary) ON employee_records TO finance_role;
Row-level security (RLS): Restrict finance to only their department's rows.

ALTER TABLE employee_records ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

-- TODO: Create a policy named finance_dept_policy
-- Requirement: finance_role can only see rows WHERE department = 'Finance'
CREATE POLICY finance_dept_policy ON employee_records
    FOR SELECT
    TO finance_role
    USING (department = 'Finance');

-- TODO: Create a similar policy for hr_role allowing full row visibility
-- TODO: Create a policy for audit_role limited to audit_flag = true rows
Verify policies exist:

SELECT * FROM pg_policies WHERE tablename = 'employee_records';
Task 3: Enable pgAudit Logging
Edit PostgreSQL configuration:

sudo find / -name "postgresql.conf" 2>/dev/null
sudo nano /etc/postgresql/15/main/postgresql.conf
Add or update these lines:

shared_preload_libraries = 'pgaudit'
pgaudit.log = 'read, write, role'
pgaudit.log_relation = on
Restart PostgreSQL:

sudo systemctl restart postgresql
Enable the extension in your database:

CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pgaudit;
Locate the log file:

sudo find /var/log/postgresql/ -name "*.log"
sudo tail -f /var/log/postgresql/postgresql-15-main.log
Task 4: Test Segregation by Impersonating Roles
Open a new terminal session and connect as each role to confirm access boundaries:

psql -U finance_role -d agency_records -h localhost
Run these queries and record the results:

-- Should succeed (allowed columns, own department only)
SELECT emp_id, full_name, department, salary FROM employee_records;

-- Should fail (ssn column not granted)
SELECT ssn FROM employee_records;

-- Should return zero rows (RLS blocks other departments)
SELECT * FROM employee_records WHERE department = 'HR';
Repeat with audit_role and hr_role. Document each result (success/denied) in a table:

Role	Query Type	Expected Result	Actual Result
finance_role	SELECT ssn	Denied	
audit_role	SELECT salary	Denied	
hr_role	SELECT *	Allowed (all rows)	
Check the pgAudit log to confirm denied attempts were recorded:

sudo grep "audit_role\|finance_role" /var/log/postgresql/postgresql-15-main.log
